DIAGNOSTIC (not for merge): trace objects_lookup, with DRM_ERROR

Replaces the previous attempt, which printed nothing. Two reasons it was
wrong:

  It used printf(), which produced no console output from this translation
  unit -- the format strings were in the built binary but never reached the
  message buffer captured in the dump. DRM_ERROR demonstrably works here;
  "[drm ERROR :vc4_fkms_bind] Oh dear, failed to register IRQ" comes out of
  the same build.

  It printed AFTER drm_gem_object_lookup() returned, which is too late. The
  backtrace lands at drm_gem_dumb_map_offset+0x2c, and disassembly puts that
  exactly at the call into objects_lookup -- so a fault inside it produces no
  output at all.

That also explains why a bogus handle survives: idr_find() returns NULL and
the loop breaks BEFORE drm_gem_object_get(). A real handle reaches the get.
DESTROY_DUMB works because drm_gem_handle_delete() takes a different path and
does not call it.

So the suspect is now drm_gem_object_get() on a real object, and this prints
the object, its device, its funcs and its refcount immediately before and
after.

Revert before merging.
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c
@@ -380,7 +380,10 @@
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	DRM_ERROR("fkms-dbg: calling create_mmap_offset, mgr=%p\n",
+	    obj->dev != NULL ? obj->dev->vma_offset_manager : NULL);
 	ret = drm_gem_create_mmap_offset(obj);
+	DRM_ERROR("fkms-dbg: create_mmap_offset ret=%d\n", ret);
 	if (ret)
 		goto out;
 
@@ -719,19 +722,27 @@
 	int i, ret = 0;
 	struct drm_gem_object *obj;
 
+	DRM_ERROR("fkms-dbg: objects_lookup filp=%p count=%d\n", filp, count);
 	spin_lock(&filp->table_lock);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
 		/* Check if we currently have a reference on the object */
 		obj = idr_find(&filp->object_idr, handle[i]);
+		DRM_ERROR("fkms-dbg:  idr_find(%u) -> %p\n", handle[i], obj);
 		if (!obj) {
 			ret = -ENOENT;
 			break;
 		}
+		DRM_ERROR("fkms-dbg:  obj dev=%p size=%zu funcs=%p refcount=%u\n",
+		    obj->dev, (size_t)obj->size, obj->funcs,
